The most prominent large-scale structure arising from this magnetic topology is the heliospheric current sheet (HCS)---the thin surface separating open field lines of opposite polarity @smithHeliosphericCurrentSheet2001. The dipole tilt and solar rotation warp this surface into a spiraling "ballerina-skirt" geometry (@fig-intro-hcs-3d) that extends throughout the heliosphere. In situ, the HCS manifests as a sharp IMF polarity reversal; Parker Solar Probe observations confirm this structure persists from close to the Sun out to 1 AU, with the large-scale geometry remaining stable while the small-scale plasma structures surrounding the sheet evolve substantially during outward propagation (@fig-intro-hcs-comparison) @szaboHeliosphericCurrentSheet2020.

