Superposed on this ordered background, however, are fluctuations spanning a broad range of scales and magnitudes. These fluctuations are a manifestation of magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) turbulence @schekochihinMHDTurbulenceBiased2022@matthaeusTurbulenceSpacePlasmas2021@brunoTurbulenceSolarWind2016@brunoSolarWindTurbulence2013@tuMHDStructuresWaves1995@goldsteinMagnetohydrodynamicTurbulenceSolar1995, which is multi-scale, intermittent, and strongly non-Gaussian @chandranIntermittentReflectiondrivenStrong2025@brunoIntermittencySolarWind2019@verscharenMultiscaleNatureSolar2019@matthaeusIntermittencyNonlinearDynamics2015. A key feature of this turbulence is that energy does not distribute uniformly across space as it cascades from large to small scales. Instead, it concentrates into localized, coherent structures --- magnetic flux ropes, magnetic holes, plasma waves, and current sheets @perroneCoherentEventsIon2020@khabarovaCurrentSheetsPlasmoids2021@pezziCurrentSheetsPlasmoids2021 --- which actively feed back into the plasma, contributing to heating, particle acceleration, and departures from classical MHD behavior @degiorgioCoherentStructureFormation2017@borovskyContributionStrongDiscontinuities2010@liEffectCurrentSheets2011@grecoPartialVarianceIncrements2017.

Among these coherent structures, current sheets---thin layers of intense electric current where the magnetic field direction changes abruptly---are the most prominent and dynamically significant (see @observations-of-solar-wind-current-sheets for a comprehensive review). First identified in Pioneer 6 measurements as sharp directional changes in the interplanetary magnetic field @nessPreliminaryResultsPioneer1966@colburnDiscontinuitiesSolarWind1966, they were initially classified within the MHD discontinuity framework as tangential discontinuities (separating topologically distinct plasma regions with no normal field component) or rotational discontinuities (propagating Alfvénic structures connecting magnetically linked regions) @hudsonDiscontinuitiesAnisotropicPlasma1970. The relative abundance of these two discontinuity types and their physical significance for magnetic topology have been subjects of longstanding debate (see @from-mhd-discontinuities-to-kinetic-scale-current-sheets) @neugebauerCommentAbundancesRotational2006. High-resolution measurements from missions such as ARTEMIS, MMS, and Parker Solar Probe have since revealed that many current sheets possess fundamentally kinetic properties: their thicknesses are on the order of a few ion inertial lengths, and their internal structure---including temperature anisotropy and non-gyrotropic ion distributions---defies a purely MHD description @artemyevKineticNatureSolar2019@vaskoKineticscaleCurrentSheets2022. Statistically, current sheets are ubiquitous throughout the heliosphere, with occurrence rates exceeding 100 events per day at 1 AU @zhangSolarWindDiscontinuities2025a (see @sec-occurrence-rate). Their normalized properties---thickness in units of the ion inertial length and current density in units of the Alfvén current density---exhibit a pronounced inverse correlation (stronger currents in thinner sheets) that extends over several orders of magnitude (see @sec-scale_density), suggesting a universal feature of the turbulent cascade @vaskoKineticScaleCurrentSheets2024. The observational characterization of these properties across the inner heliosphere is developed in @solar-wind-discontinuities-in-the-outer-heliosphere-spatial-distribution-between-1-and-5-au and @comparison-of-solar-wind-current-sheets-in-the-inner-heliosphere.

The heliosphere thus presents two interleaved levels of magnetic organization: the ordered global structure of the Parker spiral and HCS, and the smaller-scale turbulence and coherent structures embedded within that background. This multiscale magnetic environment strongly influences heliophysics processes, especially the transport of energetic particles. Large-scale topology determines magnetic connectivity while small-scale structures govern local scattering and confinement.

Solar energetic particles (SEPs) are high-energy ions and electrons episodically accelerated in the solar and interplanetary environment @anastasiadisSolarEnergeticParticles2019@kleinAccelerationPropagationSolar2017@desaiLargeGradualSolar2016@reamesTwoSourcesSolar2013 (see @energetic-particles-in-the-heliosphere). They originate either in the low solar corona during eruptive events (impulsive SEP events, associated with magnetic reconnection in solar flares), or at interplanetary shock fronts driven by fast coronal mass ejections (gradual SEP events, driven by diffusive shock acceleration) @reamesTwoSourcesSolar2013@desaiLargeGradualSolar2016. SEP events pose radiation hazards to spacecraft electronics, astronauts, and high-altitude aviation, and they provide a natural laboratory for studying particle acceleration and transport in magnetized plasmas---with implications extending to astrophysical systems more broadly.

Two contrasting observational signatures of SEP transport provide particularly compelling evidence for the role of magnetic structures in controlling particle propagation (see @reservoir-and-dropout-phenomena for a detailed discussion). The reservoir effect---in which widely separated spacecraft observe nearly identical particle intensities and spectral shapes during the decay phase of large gradual events @reamesTwoSourcesSolar2013@dallaPropertiesHighHeliolatitude2003 ---implies substantial cross-field transport that distributes particles throughout a large heliospheric volume. In contrast, dropout events---abrupt depletions of particle intensity at current sheet crossings @mazurInterplanetaryMagneticField2000@tesseinEffectCoherentStructures2015 ---indicate that particles can be effectively confined within individual magnetic flux tubes, with current sheets acting as barriers at their boundaries. Together, these phenomena reveal a fundamental duality: current sheets can both impede and facilitate particle transport, depending on their internal structure and the particle energy.

