The standard theoretical framework for SEP propagation is the quasi-linear theory (QLT) of pitch-angle scattering @jokipiiCosmicRayPropagationCharged1966 (see @turbulence-transport-frameworks), which treats the turbulent magnetic field as a superposition of small-amplitude, randomly phased waves and derives diffusion coefficients from the resonant interaction between particles and fluctuations at the cyclotron frequency. However, growing observational and numerical evidence demonstrates that the fine-scale magnetic structure of the solar wind---in particular, its coherent, intermittent character---can exert a decisive influence on particle dynamics that QLT does not capture @malaraEnergeticParticleDynamics2023@malaraChargedparticleChaoticDynamics2021@artemyevSuperfastIonScattering2020.

When a particle's gyroradius becomes comparable to the thickness of a current sheet, the standard guiding-center approximation fails because the magnetic field varies on scales smaller than the gyro-orbit. In this regime, the magnetic moment $mu$ is no longer the appropriate conserved quantity. Instead, the dynamics are governed by the quasi-adiabatic invariant $I_z$---the action variable of the fast oscillation normal to the current sheet @zelenyiQuasiadiabaticDynamicsCharged2013@buchnerRegularChaoticCharged1989 (see @quasi-adiabatic-dynamics-of-charged-particles-and-its-destruction-in-current-sheets for the full theoretical development). This invariant is approximately conserved during regular motion but undergoes jumps at separatrix crossings in phase space, where the particle transitions between qualitatively different types of oscillation (e.g., from gyration on one side of the sheet to figure-eight orbits spanning both sides). In symmetric current sheets without a guide field, these jumps are small and accumulate diffusively. However, in force-free current sheets---where the intermediate magnetic field component peaks at the center, breaking the phase-space symmetry---geometrical chaotization produces order-unity jumps in $I_z$ within a single interaction @artemyevRapidGeometricalChaotization2014@artemyevSuperfastIonScattering2020 (see @geometrical-chaotization-rapid-destruction-independent-of-kappa and @application-to-force-free-solar-wind-current-sheets-superfast-scattering). Since $I_z$ determines the particle's oscillation amplitude and thus its pitch angle, the destruction of $I_z$ is physically equivalent to pitch-angle scattering---but through a "superfast" mechanism fundamentally distinct from, and potentially more efficient than, wave--particle resonance. Because current sheets are abundant throughout the heliosphere and their thickness overlaps with the gyro-radii of suprathermal and energetic ions, such interactions are not rare events but a systematic feature of SEP propagation.

Despite their importance, a quantitative understanding of how current sheets influence SEP transport has remained incomplete, for two reasons. First, the statistical properties of kinetic-scale current sheets---their occurrence rate, thickness distribution, and internal magnetic configuration---have not been systematically characterized across different regions of the heliosphere. Second, the connection between these microphysical structural properties and macroscopic transport coefficients has not been rigorously established. This dissertation addresses both gaps.

Specifically, this dissertation (1) develops a coherent observational framework to characterize the properties and occurrence rates of current sheets across heliocentric distances from 0.17 to 5 AU @zhangSolarWindDiscontinuities2025a@zhangComparisonSolarWind2026, and (2) constructs a quantitative model of pitch-angle scattering and spatial transport of energetic particles driven by current sheets, parameterized by the observed statistical distributions @zhangQuantificationIonScattering2025. Together, these contributions bridge the microphysics of individual current sheet encounters and the macroscopic diffusion of energetic particles through the heliosphere, offering new insight into heliospheric particle dynamics and a foundation for improved space weather prediction.
